    "title": "Measuring Land with Shapes",
    "subtitle": "How farmers find the size of a field using simple shapes.",
    "body": "A farmer has a flat field. He wants to know its size. So he measures the land with a shape.\n\nMany fields are like a rectangle. A rectangle has a length and a width. To find the area, we multiply them. So area is length times width.\n\nLook at one small field. It is 4 meters long and 3 meters wide. We multiply 4 by 3. The answer is 12. So the area is 12 square meters.\n\nSome fields are not a neat rectangle. Then the farmer <mark>splits the shape</mark> into smaller parts. He finds the area of each part. Then he adds them up.\n\nThis way, any field can be measured. Math turns hard land into simple numbers.",
